1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,587
Eyelashes freeze here.

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:03,475
Cars are left running for hours.

3
00:00:03,638 --> 00:00:06,187
And around five hundred
people live here on purpose.

4
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,287
Wob has already packed a suitcase.

5
00:00:08,858 --> 00:00:12,721
This is Oymyakon, a village
in the Sakha Republic,

6
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:14,408
in the far east of Russia.

7
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:18,483
It is often called the coldest
permanently inhabited place on Earth.

8
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:21,433
Most videos about it ask
how people survive here.

9
00:00:21,608 --> 00:00:22,820
That is a fair question.

10
00:00:22,996 --> 00:00:24,496
But it skips a stranger one.

11
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:26,183
Why is there a village here at all?

12
00:00:26,345 --> 00:00:29,233
Somebody chose this spot,
in the middle of all this.

13
00:00:29,408 --> 00:00:31,696
By the end of this
video, you will know why.

14
00:00:31,858 --> 00:00:34,045
And Wob will decide whether to unpack.

15
00:00:34,208 --> 00:00:35,508
Wob's plan is simple.

16
00:00:35,733 --> 00:00:38,595
Find the chilliest village
anyone has ever heard of,

17
00:00:38,746 --> 00:00:41,433
move in, and become famous for being cold.

18
00:00:41,595 --> 00:00:45,033
Wob has packed a toothbrush,
a scarf, and a paper map.

19
00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:46,758
The map is upside down.

20
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:48,121
Wob says it is fine.

21
00:00:48,701 --> 00:00:50,063
So here is the real question.

22
00:00:50,225 --> 00:00:53,263
In a place this cold, why
would anyone stop here at all?

23
00:00:53,438 --> 00:00:55,501
Part of the answer is hiding in the name.

24
00:00:55,663 --> 00:01:00,051
Oymyakon is usually explained as
meaning water that does not freeze.

25
00:01:00,213 --> 00:01:04,738
Linguists argue about the exact roots, and
some sources trace the word to the Even

26
00:01:04,788 --> 00:01:07,076
language while others point to Sakha.

27
00:01:07,251 --> 00:01:09,951
But the story told about
this valley is the same.

28
00:01:10,113 --> 00:01:14,263
Nearby there were warm springs, water
that stayed open through the winter.

29
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:17,176
And if you move reindeer
across a frozen landscape,

30
00:01:17,313 --> 00:01:19,650
open water is not a curiosity.

31
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:20,938
It is the whole point.

32
00:01:21,101 --> 00:01:25,551
Wob, who has spent this entire trip
complaining about frozen water bottles,

33
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:27,000
suddenly understands.

34
00:01:27,163 --> 00:01:30,075
Water you can actually drink,
in the middle of winter,

35
00:01:30,263 --> 00:01:32,526
is worth walking a very long way for.

36
00:01:33,102 --> 00:01:37,477
Reindeer herders stopped at the springs to
water their animals and rest before long

37
00:01:37,527 --> 00:01:38,340
journeys.

38
00:01:38,502 --> 00:01:40,765
A stopping place became a regular camp.

39
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:44,314
Over time, the camp grew
into a permanent village.

40
00:01:44,477 --> 00:01:47,190
So Oymyakon did not
appear despite the cold.

41
00:01:47,352 --> 00:01:50,589
It appeared because, in the
middle of all that cold,

42
00:01:50,702 --> 00:01:53,314
there was one thing
that refused to freeze.

43
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:56,715
Wob, who has never herded
anything in its life,

44
00:01:56,777 --> 00:01:58,564
finds this deeply moving.

45
00:01:58,727 --> 00:02:00,440
Wob is now looking for the spring.

46
00:02:00,602 --> 00:02:02,089
Wob does not find the spring.

47
00:02:02,252 --> 00:02:04,377
Wob finds a very surprised reindeer.

48
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,040
So how cold is this cold, exactly?

49
00:02:07,202 --> 00:02:10,028
On the sixth of February,
nineteen thirty-three,

50
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:14,340
the weather station here recorded about
minus sixty-eight degrees Celsius.

51
00:02:14,515 --> 00:02:16,627
That is about minus ninety Fahrenheit.

52
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:20,915
Guinness World Records lists it
as minus sixty-seven point seven.

53
00:02:21,090 --> 00:02:25,915
The World Meteorological Organization
lists minus sixty-seven point eight.

54
00:02:26,078 --> 00:02:28,590
So even the number itself
comes with an argument.

55
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:32,802
Either way, it is the lowest temperature
on record for a place where people live all

56
00:02:32,852 --> 00:02:33,652
year.

57
00:02:33,615 --> 00:02:34,415
Well.

58
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:35,090
Almost.

59
00:02:35,177 --> 00:02:40,040
Far to the northwest, the town of
Verkhoyansk has its own reading from eighteen

60
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:42,552
ninety-two, of about the same number.

61
00:02:42,728 --> 00:02:45,278
Some sources say the two
towns share the record.

62
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,065
Others treat the Verkhoyansk
reading as disputed.

63
00:02:49,228 --> 00:02:50,228
Rival towns.

64
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:51,490
A tenth of a degree.

65
00:02:51,652 --> 00:02:53,390
More than a century of arguing.

66
00:02:53,552 --> 00:02:55,415
Wob finds this very relatable.

67
00:02:55,577 --> 00:02:57,953
Wob once argued with a fridge for an hour.

68
00:02:58,535 --> 00:03:01,122
There is a second argument,
much closer to home.

69
00:03:01,297 --> 00:03:05,472
The weather station behind that famous
number is not in Oymyakon itself.

70
00:03:05,635 --> 00:03:10,160
It sits in a valley between Oymyakon and
a neighbouring village called Tomtor,

71
00:03:10,222 --> 00:03:11,697
near Tomtor's airfield.

72
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:13,660
Tomtor would like you to know this.

73
00:03:13,834 --> 00:03:17,222
Some sources argue that
Tomtor, not Oymyakon,

74
00:03:17,372 --> 00:03:19,010
is the real Pole of Cold.

75
00:03:19,172 --> 00:03:22,547
Oymyakon, meanwhile, has
the official monument.

76
00:03:22,722 --> 00:03:25,984
So Wob, who wants to move
somewhere truly freezing,

77
00:03:26,110 --> 00:03:29,684
now has to choose between the village
with the monument and the village with the

78
00:03:29,734 --> 00:03:30,610
thermometer.

79
00:03:30,772 --> 00:03:32,072
Wob suggests sharing.

80
00:03:32,234 --> 00:03:33,559
Nobody is interested.

81
00:03:33,722 --> 00:03:37,122
It is very cold outside,
and nobody is backing down.

82
00:03:37,284 --> 00:03:40,059
Wob tries standing exactly
halfway between them.

83
00:03:40,222 --> 00:03:42,709
Halfway turns out to be
mostly a frozen road.

84
00:03:43,285 --> 00:03:45,360
But why is it so cold here to begin with?

85
00:03:45,522 --> 00:03:48,584
Oymyakon is not even
inside the Arctic Circle.

86
00:03:48,747 --> 00:03:51,334
It sits a few degrees
of latitude south of it.

87
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:53,084
The cold is a team effort.

88
00:03:53,322 --> 00:03:58,222
First, in winter a huge, stable zone of
high pressure called the Siberian High

89
00:03:58,272 --> 00:04:00,322
settles over this part of Asia.

90
00:04:00,484 --> 00:04:05,697
It brings clear, dry skies, and clear skies
let the ground lose its heat straight up

91
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:06,785
into space.

92
00:04:06,947 --> 00:04:09,209
Second, there is the valley itself.

93
00:04:09,372 --> 00:04:10,447
Cold air is heavy.

94
00:04:10,622 --> 00:04:13,672
It slides downhill and
pools on the valley floor,

95
00:04:13,722 --> 00:04:17,710
like water in a bowl, while the
mountains around it keep warmer air out.

96
00:04:17,872 --> 00:04:22,097
Researchers measuring this valley have found
the mountain slopes above it can be far

97
00:04:22,147 --> 00:04:24,484
warmer in winter than the valley floor.

98
00:04:24,659 --> 00:04:27,272
So the village sits at the
bottom of a giant bowl,

99
00:04:27,384 --> 00:04:30,097
full of the heaviest,
chilliest air around.

100
00:04:30,259 --> 00:04:31,647
Wob would like a smaller bowl.

101
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,134
Wob briefly considers
climbing out of the bowl.

102
00:04:34,297 --> 00:04:35,872
Up on the slopes it is warmer.

103
00:04:36,034 --> 00:04:37,210
It is also a mountain.

104
00:04:37,372 --> 00:04:38,610
Wob stays in the bowl.

105
00:04:39,182 --> 00:04:42,745
In the Soviet era, the state
pushed deep into this region.

106
00:04:42,907 --> 00:04:44,470
A weather station was set up.

107
00:04:44,619 --> 00:04:46,707
An airfield was built near Tomtor.

108
00:04:46,869 --> 00:04:50,594
And the record that made Oymyakon famous
came from that station in nineteen

109
00:04:50,644 --> 00:04:51,469
thirty-three.

110
00:04:51,632 --> 00:04:56,457
In other words, Oymyakon became famous for
its cold partly because someone finally

111
00:04:56,507 --> 00:04:57,857
put a thermometer there.

112
00:04:58,032 --> 00:05:02,344
Thermometers, it turns out, are
very good at making places famous.

113
00:05:02,507 --> 00:05:04,144
Wob makes a note to pack one.

114
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:08,046
This was also one of the darkest
periods in the region's history.

115
00:05:08,208 --> 00:05:12,608
In the nineteen thirties, the Soviet Union
built a network of forced labour camps

116
00:05:12,658 --> 00:05:15,858
across the Kolyma region,
part of the Gulag system,

117
00:05:15,971 --> 00:05:17,446
largely to mine gold.

118
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:21,770
Prisoners began building the Kolyma
Highway in the early nineteen thirties,

119
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:24,958
and forced labour continued on
it into the nineteen fifties.

120
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:26,771
Many of them did not survive.

121
00:05:26,945 --> 00:05:29,433
The village's story
begins with the springs.

122
00:05:29,608 --> 00:05:33,758
But the highway that crosses this region
was built by people who had no choice.

123
00:05:34,338 --> 00:05:35,888
So what is daily life like?

124
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:40,638
In January, the average temperature
here is around minus forty-six Celsius.

125
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,800
In late December the sun is up for
only about four and a half hours a day.

126
00:05:44,976 --> 00:05:48,176
That leaves very little time
to do anything outdoors,

127
00:05:48,226 --> 00:05:51,100
which, in this weather,
may be for the best.

128
00:05:51,276 --> 00:05:53,950
Beneath the surface the
ground is permafrost,

129
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,838
frozen all year, which makes ordinary
water pipes very hard to keep working.

130
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,900
Many homes use outdoor toilets.

131
00:06:01,063 --> 00:06:02,788
Yes, even in January.

132
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:07,500
Cars are often left running for hours,
or warmed with a fire before starting,

133
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:10,663
because a frozen engine
may refuse to start at all.

134
00:06:11,328 --> 00:06:14,078
Food comes mostly from
animals and the river:

135
00:06:14,265 --> 00:06:19,065
meat, and fish that in winter is sold
frozen solid and stacked like firewood.

136
00:06:19,302 --> 00:06:22,165
Farming in frozen ground
is close to impossible.

137
00:06:22,328 --> 00:06:24,740
Wob decides to test
all of this personally.

138
00:06:24,902 --> 00:06:26,127
Wob steps outside.

139
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:28,378
Wob does not technically have eyelashes.

140
00:06:28,553 --> 00:06:29,765
They freeze anyway.

141
00:06:29,928 --> 00:06:32,528
A few minutes later, Wob is back indoors,

142
00:06:32,578 --> 00:06:34,878
holding a cup of tea with both hands,

143
00:06:34,928 --> 00:06:37,552
quietly reconsidering
several life choices.

144
00:06:37,727 --> 00:06:41,415
The tea, meanwhile, is
cooling at an alarming speed.

145
00:06:41,578 --> 00:06:42,828
Wob drinks it quickly.

146
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:44,252
Wob regrets that too.

147
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:47,368
Children here are tough,
but not unlimited.

148
00:06:47,530 --> 00:06:51,717
According to Russian news reports, younger
pupils in the district get the day off

149
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:53,955
when it reaches around minus fifty-two.

150
00:06:54,117 --> 00:06:57,542
The exact cut-off for each age
group depends on who you ask.

151
00:06:57,730 --> 00:07:02,142
For comparison, plenty of schools elsewhere
close for a light dusting of snow.

152
00:07:02,305 --> 00:07:04,855
Wob asks whether Oymyakon has snow days.

153
00:07:05,030 --> 00:07:06,205
The answer is yes.

154
00:07:06,367 --> 00:07:08,280
They are just extremely specific.

155
00:07:08,844 --> 00:07:10,319
And then, the twist.

156
00:07:10,482 --> 00:07:12,544
Oymyakon is not cold all year.

157
00:07:12,719 --> 00:07:15,594
Summers are short, but
they can be properly warm.

158
00:07:15,794 --> 00:07:18,707
For a few weeks, the
frozen valley turns green.

159
00:07:18,881 --> 00:07:22,294
By late June, daylight lasts
more than twenty hours.

160
00:07:22,457 --> 00:07:27,494
In July twenty ten, the station recorded
thirty-four point six degrees Celsius.

161
00:07:27,669 --> 00:07:29,594
That is about ninety-four Fahrenheit.

162
00:07:29,769 --> 00:07:32,794
Put the record low and the
record high side by side,

163
00:07:32,919 --> 00:07:35,781
and the gap is roughly one
hundred and two degrees.

164
00:07:35,944 --> 00:07:40,144
Very few places on Earth have record
highs and lows that far apart.

165
00:07:40,319 --> 00:07:44,606
Wob, who packed nothing but fur,
is suddenly optimistic again.

166
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:46,558
So why do people stay now?

167
00:07:46,758 --> 00:07:49,120
For many families, this is simply home.

168
00:07:49,283 --> 00:07:51,745
Some of the toughest
locals are not even human.

169
00:07:51,908 --> 00:07:55,632
Yakutian horses live outdoors
all year in this region,

170
00:07:55,770 --> 00:07:56,920
right through the winter.

171
00:07:57,082 --> 00:08:01,032
A genetic study published in twenty fifteen
found that they descend from domestic

172
00:08:01,082 --> 00:08:03,970
horses brought here only
a few centuries ago.

173
00:08:04,132 --> 00:08:07,032
They adapted to the cold remarkably fast,

174
00:08:07,082 --> 00:08:10,395
with compact bodies and
very thick winter coats.

175
00:08:10,570 --> 00:08:13,782
Wob, whose only adaptation
is a large backpack,

176
00:08:13,832 --> 00:08:14,832
is impressed.

177
00:08:15,008 --> 00:08:16,808
Wob tries to make friends with one.

178
00:08:16,983 --> 00:08:18,508
The horse is unimpressed.

179
00:08:18,682 --> 00:08:21,045
The horse has clearly
seen tourists before.

180
00:08:21,208 --> 00:08:22,657
Not everyone stays, though.

181
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:26,033
Life at the Pole of Cold is a
choice that families keep making,

182
00:08:26,083 --> 00:08:27,582
or don't, every year.

183
00:08:28,144 --> 00:08:30,831
And the cold itself has
become a reason to visit.

184
00:08:30,994 --> 00:08:35,069
Every spring, the district hosts a
festival called the Pole of Cold,

185
00:08:35,182 --> 00:08:38,819
with events in villages
including Oymyakon and Tomtor.

186
00:08:38,982 --> 00:08:42,557
Its star is Chyskhaan,
the Sakha Lord of Cold,

187
00:08:42,681 --> 00:08:45,106
who reportedly has his own ice residence.

188
00:08:45,269 --> 00:08:48,232
In twenty twenty-five, there
was even a marathon here.

189
00:08:48,394 --> 00:08:49,232
In the cold.

190
00:08:49,394 --> 00:08:50,282
On purpose.

191
00:08:50,431 --> 00:08:54,669
A village that grew up around water that
wouldn't freeze now welcomes visitors

192
00:08:54,719 --> 00:08:56,631
because of everything that does.

193
00:08:56,794 --> 00:08:59,969
Visitors come to stand next
to the Pole of Cold monument,

194
00:09:00,019 --> 00:09:02,494
take a photo, and hurry back indoors.

195
00:09:02,669 --> 00:09:03,744
So let's add it all up.

196
00:09:03,919 --> 00:09:07,019
People stopped here because of
water that would not freeze,

197
00:09:07,144 --> 00:09:09,669
in a land where almost everything does.

198
00:09:09,832 --> 00:09:11,782
A stopping place became a village.

199
00:09:11,944 --> 00:09:14,232
A weather station gave
it a famous reading,

200
00:09:14,381 --> 00:09:17,219
and that reading gave it a
name the whole world knows.

201
00:09:17,381 --> 00:09:20,982
The highway that crosses the region
carries a much darker history.

202
00:09:21,156 --> 00:09:25,694
And today, the families who live here stay
for reasons that are easy to understand:

203
00:09:25,869 --> 00:09:29,457
home, the school, the
herds, the weather station,

204
00:09:29,606 --> 00:09:31,707
and, lately, visitors.

205
00:09:31,881 --> 00:09:33,694
The cold was never the reason to come.

206
00:09:33,856 --> 00:09:35,232
It was the price of staying.

207
00:09:35,814 --> 00:09:37,127
So, would Wob move here?

208
00:09:37,301 --> 00:09:38,514
The springs are lovely.

209
00:09:38,689 --> 00:09:40,701
The summers are a genuine surprise.

210
00:09:40,864 --> 00:09:45,076
And the people of Oymyakon have built a
real home in one of the hardest places on

211
00:09:45,126 --> 00:09:45,926
the planet.

212
00:09:46,089 --> 00:09:49,564
But the winters here are
long, dark and brutally cold,

213
00:09:49,689 --> 00:09:51,326
and Wob packed for a weekend.

214
00:09:51,501 --> 00:09:52,652
The verdict is NOPE.

215
00:09:52,827 --> 00:09:55,139
This is a place for people
who grew up with it,

216
00:09:55,301 --> 00:09:58,839
or who truly, deeply love winter: silence,

217
00:09:58,889 --> 00:10:01,714
clear skies, and a
summer that feels earned.

218
00:10:01,877 --> 00:10:03,039
Wob is unpacking.

219
00:10:03,202 --> 00:10:04,002
Slowly.

220
00:10:04,026 --> 00:10:04,989
With gloves on.

221
00:10:05,152 --> 00:10:08,176
Wob will be following the thermometer
from somewhere much warmer.

222
00:10:08,339 --> 00:10:09,377
So, be honest.

223
00:10:09,539 --> 00:10:11,064
How long would you last out there?
